{
  "title": "Remember recently played games and surface them above the cabinet grid",
  "open_questions": [
    {
      "id": "what-counts-as-played",
      "question": "Does a game count as 'played' the moment someone opens it from the cabinet, or only after they actually start a round (e.g. press start / the game begins)?",
      "required": true
    },
    {
      "id": "retired-game-in-history",
      "question": "If someone's recent list contains a game we have since retired, should it disappear silently (and the next most recent game take its place), or stay visible but clearly unavailable?",
      "required": true
    }
  ],
  "criteria": [
    {
      "id": "recent-section-above-grid",
      "title": "A recently played section appears above the full grid",
      "guidance": "Load the cabinet page for a visitor whose stored history contains one or more games. A distinct section renders before (above) the existing grid of all games, introduced by a heading that reads as the visitor's own history rather than a suggestion. Decision taken unless overruled: the heading text is 'Recently played'. Checkable by rendering the page markup in a test and asserting the section and heading exist and precede the full grid.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    },
    {
      "id": "ordering-most-recent-first",
      "title": "Recent entries are listed most recent first",
      "guidance": "Record plays of game A, then B, then C and render. The section lists C, B, A in that order. Checkable by asserting the order of the rendered entries.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    },
    {
      "id": "replay-moves-to-front",
      "title": "Replaying a game moves it to the front instead of duplicating it",
      "guidance": "Record A, B, then A again. The section shows A, B — A appears exactly once and is first. Assert no game id appears twice in the rendered section.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    },
    {
      "id": "cap-at-three",
      "title": "At most three games are shown",
      "guidance": "Record four or more distinct plays. The section renders exactly three entries, the three most recently played. Older entries are dropped rather than shown. Assert the rendered count is three and the fourth-oldest game is absent.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    },
    {
      "id": "persists-across-sessions",
      "title": "History survives closing the tab",
      "guidance": "History is written to durable per-browser storage (localStorage or equivalent), not to memory or a session-only store. Decision taken unless overruled: history is per-browser and anonymous — there are no accounts and no syncing between devices. Checkable by recording plays, discarding the in-memory state, re-reading storage and rendering the same list.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    },
    {
      "id": "full-catalogue-still-listed",
      "title": "The full grid still lists every game, unchanged",
      "guidance": "With and without history present, the existing grid below still contains every game the cabinet knows about, in its current order, including any game that also appears in the recent section (entries are duplicated, not moved). Assert the grid's game ids match the full catalogue in both cases.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    },
    {
      "id": "single-source-of-truth-for-games",
      "title": "The recent section reads game metadata from the existing catalogue",
      "guidance": "The stored history holds game identifiers only; titles, links and any artwork for the recent entries are looked up from the repository's existing catalogue module rather than a second hardcoded list. I have not read src/catalogue.js, so the implementer must adopt whatever identifier and shape it already exposes. Checkable by inspecting the diff and by a test that adding or renaming a catalogue entry changes the rendered recent entry with no other edit.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    },
    {
      "id": "no-history-no-section",
      "title": "A first-time visitor sees today's page exactly",
      "guidance": "Decision taken unless overruled: with no stored history the recent section is not rendered at all — no heading, no empty placeholder — so a first visit looks exactly as the cabinet does today. Assert the section is absent from the rendered markup when storage is empty.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    },
    {
      "id": "survives-unusable-storage-state",
      "title": "Corrupt or unexpected stored data cannot break the cabinet",
      "guidance": "With storage holding malformed JSON, a non-array value, entries missing fields, entries of the wrong type, or with storage throwing on read or write (private mode / quota), the page still renders, the full grid is intact and every game link still works. The bad value is ignored or replaced rather than propagated. Checkable by driving the render with each of those stored values and asserting no exception escapes and the grid is complete.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    },
    {
      "id": "unknown-game-ids-tolerated",
      "title": "History referencing a game that no longer exists degrades quietly",
      "guidance": "When history contains an identifier that is not in the catalogue, the page still renders and all remaining games still start; the unknown entry is handled per the answer to the retired-game question, and until that answer arrives the default is to skip it and fill from the next most recent known game. Checkable by seeding history with a bogus id alongside real ones and asserting the render succeeds and lists the real games.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    },
    {
      "id": "recording-cannot-block-play",
      "title": "Recording a play never prevents a game from starting",
      "guidance": "If writing history fails or throws for any reason, launching the game still proceeds. Checkable by stubbing the write to throw and asserting the launch path completes without error.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    },
    {
      "id": "section-reads-as-yours",
      "title": "The heading and section read as the visitor's own history",
      "guidance": "A person reads the rendered page and judges that the section is unmistakably 'games you played', not a recommendation or a promotion, and that three entries above the grid feel like a shortcut rather than a second grid. Judgement call on wording and weight; no automated check.",
      "required": true,
      "human": true
    }
  ],
  "gates": [
    {"id": "lint", "run": "npm run lint"},
    {"id": "test", "run": "npm test"}
  ],
  "gate_bindings": [
    {
      "id": "acceptance-recently-played",
      "run": "node --test acceptance/recently-played.test.js",
      "proves": "recent-section-above-grid"
    }
  ],
  "tasks": [
    {
      "id": "history-store",
      "brief": "briefs/history-store.md",
      "dir": ".",
      "objective": "Build the module that remembers what has been played: a small store that records a play by game identifier, keeps the list most-recent-first, moves a replayed game to the front instead of duplicating it, keeps at most three entries, and persists to durable per-browser storage so it survives closing the tab. Every read is defensive — malformed JSON, non-array values, wrong-typed or incomplete entries, and storage that throws on read or write all resolve to an empty history rather than an error, and a failed write is swallowed so recording can never block a game from starting. Done when the store's behaviour is covered by tests alongside the repository's existing ones and both existing gates stay green."
    },
    {
      "id": "cabinet-section",
      "brief": "briefs/cabinet-section.md",
      "dir": ".",
      "objective": "Render the recent games in the cabinet. Read the history, resolve each identifier against the existing catalogue module (adopting whatever id and shape it already exposes, with no second hardcoded game list), drop or mark identifiers the catalogue does not know per the agreed behaviour, and render at most three entries under a 'Recently played' heading in a section that sits above the existing full grid. When history is empty the section is not rendered at all. The full grid below is untouched: it still lists every game in its current order, including games that also appear above. Done when the page renders correctly for empty, partial, full and stale histories and both existing gates stay green."
    },
    {
      "id": "record-on-launch",
      "brief": "briefs/record-on-launch.md",
      "dir": ".",
      "objective": "Wire recording into the launch path so that playing a game updates the history at the moment agreed in the answer to the 'what counts as played' question, using the same identifier the catalogue uses. Recording is best-effort: any failure is caught and the game still opens. Done when launching each of the games in the repository produces a history entry that the cabinet section then shows first on the next render, launching still works with storage disabled or throwing, and both existing gates stay green."
    },
    {
      "id": "resilience-acceptance",
      "brief": "briefs/resilience-acceptance.md",
      "dir": ".",
      "objective": "Prove the shortcut cannot take the arcade down. Exercise the cabinet against the full range of hostile stored states — absent, malformed JSON, non-array, entries of the wrong type, entries naming a retired game, and storage that throws on read and on write — and assert in each case that the page renders, the full grid contains every catalogue game and every game link still points at a launchable game. Fill out the existing acceptance/recently-played.test.js so it covers ordering, de-duplication, the three-entry cap, persistence across a fresh read and these failure modes end to end. Done when that file passes with the feature in place and both existing gates stay green."
    }
  ]
}
